a suggests model_id per variant. Alternatively, use matrix_tables to preserve tables and populate model_level with attribute × model matrix.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"Lists of values... is a MODEL MATRIX — emit model_level entries\". Here Max varies per model variant. The cleanest approach:\n\n1. Preserve the 6 tables as matrix_tables with name \"Single-interval instruments\" / \"Dual-interval instruments\", rows as objects.\n\n2. model_family.models: enumerate variants. That could be many: ED single 4, ER PLUS single 3, ER PLUS M single 3, SW-1 group single 8×5=40, ER JR single 3, PW-II single 3 = 61 single. Dual: ED 4, ER PLUS/M 6, SW-1 40, ER JR 3, PW-II 3 = 56. Total 117 variants. That's a lot but doable programmatically... but I'm writing JSON by hand. That's excessive.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: define models at the model-name level (ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II) with attributes capturing the ranges/sets, and put detailed per-capacity data in matrix_tables, and model_level characteristics mapping each model name to its set of Max values.\n\nHmm. The schema says model_level: attribute × model matrix with values[] each {model, value}. If a model has multiple Max values (variants), the value could be a list? The rules say lists of values should be a model matrix, not list-as-value. But here the granularity is sub-model variants.\n\nI think the best balance: model_id per variant like \"ED (single-interval, Max 3 kg)\". But 117 entries is huge. Let me reconsider.\n\nPerhaps a middle ground: model entries per model-name × capacity for single-interval and dual-interval combined, e.g. model_id \"ED-3/6\" no...\n\nActually, let me think about what's most useful and schema-conformant:\n\nOption A: models[] with model_id like \"ED Max 3 kg (single-interval)\" — enumerate all. Too many, error-prone.\n\nOption B: models[] at the model-name level (10 entries: ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II), each with attributes noting single/dual interval availability. Then model_level characteristics: \"maximum_capacity\" with values per model as list of values? That violates \"numbers not strings\" but value can be an array? Schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. No arrays.\n\nOption C: Use config_level with axis \"interval_configuration\" (single vs dual) and matrix_tables for the detailed tables. And model_level at model-name level with e.g. \"max_capacity_range\"? Not great either.\n\nGiven rule 4's R137 example: explicit 2-D table → matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models AND model_level. Here the 2-D tables are transposed: rows are attributes (Max, Min, e), columns are model variants. The \"models\" are the columns.\n\nI'll go with a hybrid:\n- model_family.family_name: \"SW Series\"\n- models: entries per model variant grouped... \n\nLet me count more carefully and decide. Actually enumerating all 117 is mechanical; I could do it but risk errors and huge output. The task says \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" Quality over exhaustiveness; but matrix detection is \"MOST IMPORTANT\". \n\nI think a reasonable, defensible extraction:\n\nmatrix_tables: 6 tables (3 single-interval, 3 dual-interval), each with name, columns (model variant columns), rows (Max, Min, e). For the SW-1 table the columns are 8 capacities under one model group spanning 5 names.\n\nFor model_level characteristics: attribute \"maximum_capacity\" per model at the model-name level where each value is the set... no.\n\nAlternative: treat each (model, capacity) as a variant in models[] but compress by using model_id like \"ED-3\" etc. Let me enumerate:\n\nSingle-interval:\nED: Max 3 (Min 20, e 1), Max 6 (Min 40, e 2), Max 15 (Min 100, e 5), Max 30 (Min 200, e 10) → 4\nER PLUS: 6 (40, 2), 15 (100, 5), 30 (200, 10) → 3\nER PLUS M: same 3\nSW-1S/C/W/LR/WR: 2 (20,1), 3 (20,1), 5 (40,2), 6 (40,2), 10 (100,5), 15 (100,5), 20 (200,10), 30 (200,10) → 8 × 5 = 40\nER JR: 6 (40,2), 15 (100,5), 30 (200,10) → 3\nPW-II: 2 (20,1), 5 (40,2), 10 (100,5) → 3\n\nDual-interval:\nED: 1.5/3 (10, 0.5/1), 3/6 (20, 1/2), 6/15 (40, 2/5), 15/30 (100, 5/10) → 4\nER PLUS/M: 3/6 (20,1/2), 6/15 (40,2/5), 15/30 (100,5/10) → 3 ×2 = 6\nSW-1 group: 1/2 (10,0.5/1), 1.5/3 (10,0.5/1), 2.5/5 (20,1/2), 3/6 (20,1/2), 4/10 (40,2/5), 6/15 (40,2/5), 10/20 (100,5/10), 15/30 (100,5/10) → 8 × 5 = 40\nER JR: 3/6 (20,1/2), 6/15 (40,2/5), 15/30 (100,5/10) → 3\nPW-II: 1/2 (10,0.5/1), 2.5/5 (20,1/2), 4/10 (40,2/5) → 3\n\nTotal single 56? Let me recount single: 4+3+3+40+3+3 = 56. Dual: 4+6+40+3+3 = 56. Total 112.\n\n112 model entries is a lot but each is small. Hmm. Writing all 112 entries by hand invites errors.\n\nCompromise: models[] at model-name level with attributes: {\"interval_configuration\": [\"single\", \"dual\"], \"maximum_capacity_values_kg\": [...]}. That's off-schema.\n\nBetter compromise: Put the full detail in matrix_tables (preserving tables faithfully), and in model_family.models list model-name-level entries (11 entries: ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II). Then model_level characteristics:\n\n- maximum_capacity: values per model as lists? Not allowed as list...\n\nHmm, actually the schema for model_level values[] entries: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. value: number|range|string. For model ED (single), Max ∈ {3,6,15,30}. Could encode as value string \"3/6/15/30\"? Ugly.\n\nAlternatively, define the axis as capacity variant: config_level with attribute \"maximum_capacity\", axis \"model × capacity variant\". Config level: {condition, value}. condition: \"ED, single-interval, Max 3 kg\"? Redundant.\n\nI think the cleanest schema-true approach that stays readable: enumerate model variants in models[] with model_id encoding model + capacity + interval config, and model_level entries for maximum_capacity, minimum_capacity, verification_scale_interval