ybe model_level with model = model_id of the enumerated variant, but I only enumerate variants... circular.\n\nNew idea: Use model_level where \"model\" values are the column identifiers from the tables, e.g. \"ED (single, Max 3kg)\". And models[] lists these same ids. Then matrix_tables redundant but preserving original layout. That is schema-conformant and complete. Count: 112.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the schema for model_family: \"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\". And models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. The attributes{} is a free-form object — I can put max/min/e in attributes per model. Then model_level[] separately? That duplicates.\n\nEfficient plan:\n- models[]: 112 entries each with attributes {maximum_capacity: {value, unit}, minimum_capacity, verification_scale_interval, weighing_mode}. Too big.\n\nAlternatively models[] with 112 entries each just model_id and label, and model_level[] with 3 attributes × 112 values. Also big.\n\nLet me estimate JSON size: 112 models entries × ~60 chars = ~7KB; model_level 3×112 × ~55 = ~18KB. Total ~25KB. That's large but acceptable? The response would be long. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, compress: models at model-name level (10 entries) with attributes containing arrays for capacities:\n\n{\"model_id\": \"ED\", \"label\": \"ED\", \"attributes\": {\"interval_configurations\": [\"single\", \"dual\"], \"single_interval\": {\"maximum_capacity_kg\": [3,6,15,30], \"minimum_capacity_g\": [20,40,100,200], \"verification_scale_interval_g\": [1,2,5,10]}, \"dual_interval\": {...}}}\n\nThis deviates from structured-value rules but keeps info. The instructions are strict about characteristics values, but models[].attributes is loosely defined (\"attributes{}\").\n\nGiven the tension, I'll take a pragmatic approach prioritizing correctness and completeness of data with reasonable schema adherence:\n\n1. certificate: number \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\", revision? The schema doesn't have revision field for certificate